A representative firm hires labor and rents capital to solve
\[
\max_{k \geq 0, \ell \geq 0} k^{\alpha} \ell^{1-\alpha} - rk - w\ell,
\]
where $\alpha \in (0, 1)$ is the capital share. Firm optimization requires
\begin{align}
k &= K(r, \ell) \equiv \left( \frac{\alpha}{r} \right)^{\frac{1}{\alpha}} \ell^{1-\alpha} \tag{2.3} \\
w &= (1 - \alpha) \left( \frac{\alpha}{r} \right)^{\frac{1-\alpha}{\alpha}}. \tag{2.4}
\end{align}
This setup is the canonical Aiyagari (1994) model, and it satisfies Assumptions 1–5 of Açıkgöz (2018), which he uses to establish all of his results I use below.

As the difference in random shocks becomes more dispersed (captured by a higher \(\sigma\)), performance evaluation becomes noisier, so the manager has weaker incentives to invest effort when the head start favors her (\(h \geq 0\)). A noisier performance evaluation also makes the manager less concerned about competing with a higher-ability agent, leading her to hire a higher-ability candidate. The variance affects the new hire’s effort through two channels: on the one hand, it discourages effort by making performance evaluation noisier; on the other, it results in a higher-ability new hire, which in turn increases his effort. Overall, the net effect on the new hire’s effort is positive.

\begin{footnotesize}
\begin{enumerate}
